    "body": "The consumer side of [Adapton/fumola#69](https://github.com/Adapton/fumola/issues/69) \u2014 the runtime is published to content-addressed URLs by [Adapton/fumola#76](https://github.com/Adapton/fumola/pull/76), and this reads the manifest that names them.\n\nTargets `fumola-livelit-mvp` rather than `dev`, since that is where the Fumola livelit work lives.\n\n**Order does not matter.** An origin with no `runtime.json` is exactly the fallback path. Verified against the live `fumola.org` today, which has none: the loader falls back, reports `fumola.org @ stable`, and evaluates correctly. So this can merge before, after, or independently of the Fumola side.\n\n### The problem\n\nThe published runtime is served from stable URLs and GitHub Pages stamps `Cache-Control: max-age=600` on everything with no way to change it. So a Fumola deploy cannot invalidate the 5.6 MB binary a browser already holds, and a livelit can run against a runtime a version behind \u2014 which looks like a bug rather than a cache.\n\n### What changed in `prebundle.js`\n\nReads `<origin>/runtime.json` before importing, and takes the glue and the binary from the directory it names. Both or neither \u2014 wasm-bindgen emits them as a matched set and they do not load if their versions disagree, so a half-applied manifest would be worse than ignoring one.\n\nA published origin stays **one** source with one timeout budget rather than becoming two entries; doubling the source count would double the worst case a page waits before reporting the runtime unavailable (currently 90s across three).\n\n### `window.fumola.source()` now says which version\n\n```\n\"fumola.org @ 290846d3aad19d38\"   the current build, by content hash\n\"fumola.org @ stable\"             manifest unreachable; the root pair\n\"local @ stable\"                  your own scripts/build-fumola-wasm.sh\n```\n\nPreviously unanswerable from a browser, and it is the first question worth asking when a livelit misbehaves for someone and not for you. It also removes the need for a `fumola_version()` export compiled into the wasm \u2014 the manifest is the version stamp.\n\n### Verification\n\nDriven in a browser with the **real** bundle (esbuild over `prebundle.js`, same as the dune rule):\n\n| Scenario | Result |\n| --- | --- |\n| Live `fumola.org` (no manifest today) | falls back, `fumola.org @ stable`, `` `x := 41; get(`x) + 1 `` \u2192 42 |\n| Locally published site with a manifest | requests `runtime.json`, then the hashed glue and binary; `source()` reports the hash; \u2192 42 |\n\nPlus `dune build src/web/www/bundled.js`, and `FumolaValue` 54/54, `FumolaSource` 21/21 \u2014 though see below on what those actually cover.\n\n### `docs/fumola-runtime-changes.md`\n\nNew, and the reason this was looked at. Hazel pins no version of Fumola \u2014 no submodule, no revision, no lockfile entry \u2014 so **a change merged there reaches every Hazel user without any commit here, and nothing in Hazel's CI notices.** The doc records the dependency precisely (including that the bridge calls only 6 of the runtime's 17 exports, which is what makes \"did that Fumola change break us?\" a two-minute check), an event log starting with fumola#74, and a four-tier testing methodology.\n\nIts least comfortable finding, stated plainly because green tests are easy to mistake for coverage here: **nothing in either repository tests a Fumola livelit against a live runtime.** `Evaluator.Livelit` skips all four outright; `FumolaValue` and `FumolaSource` feed hand-written JSON fixtures and would pass with the wasm deleted; `prebundle.js` has no test of any kind; there is no headless harness. Those fixtures are the only written record of the two repos' agreement and they are transcribed by hand, so a reply-shape change in Fumola leaves them passing while asserting the old shape. Generating them from the runtime is the first thing that would close it, and needs no browser.\n\n\ud83e\udd16 Generated with [Claude Code](https://claude.com/claude-code)\n",
